Here's a plan for a daily motivation site, plus prompts you can plug into an LLM and (optionally) an image model.

Core concept

A site that gives each visitor a fresh, personalized dose of motivation daily — quote/message + optional visual — with light gamification (streaks) so people come back.

1. Architecture

Frontend

Next.js or SvelteKit — fast, SEO-friendly, good for a content-refreshing homepage
Hero section: today's message, updates once daily (server-side, cached)
Optional: mood/goal picker so content feels personalized ("I need motivation for: fitness / career / studying / general")
Streak tracker (visits in a row) stored in localStorage or tied to an account
Shareable card (generate an image of today's quote for social sharing)

Backend

Node/Express or Python FastAPI
Cron job (daily, e.g. midnight UTC or per-user timezone) to generate/select the day's content
Database: Postgres — tables for messages, categories, users (if accounts), streaks
Cache layer (Redis or just DB flag) so the same message serves all users that day without regenerating per-request

Content strategy — pick one:

Curated bank: pre-written quotes/messages in DB, rotate daily (simplest, cheapest, most reliable)
LLM-generated: generate a new message daily via API call in the cron job (fresher, more flexible, small ongoing cost)
Hybrid (recommended): curated bank as fallback + LLM generates variation/personalization on top

Personalization (optional, phase 2)

Let users pick categories or input a goal ("I'm training for a marathon")
Store lightly, use it to bias which message/category is shown
2. LLM prompt (daily message generation)
You are writing a short daily motivational message for a website. Today's 
date is {date}. The user's selected focus area is: {category, e.g. "career", 
"fitness", "general life", "creativity"}.

Write:
1. A short headline (max 8 words) — punchy, present-tense, not clichéd
2. A 2-3 sentence message (encouraging, grounded, avoids toxic positivity — 
   acknowledge effort/struggle, not just "you got this!")
3. One small, concrete action the person could take today related to this 
   theme (max 15 words)

Avoid: generic phrases like "believe in yourself," overused quote-poster 
language, religious references, guilt-tripping tone.
Tone: warm, honest, a little bit real — like a good friend, not a poster.

Output as JSON:
{ "headline": "", "message": "", "action": "" }
3. Image prompt (for shareable daily card)
Minimalist motivational quote card, clean modern typography, soft gradient 
background in [calming color palette — e.g. warm sunrise tones / soft blues], 
plenty of negative space, no clutter, aspect ratio 1:1 for social sharing.

Text overlay (added separately, not baked into image generation): 
"{headline}"

Bake the text in with CSS/Canvas rather than the image model — sharper, more legible, and you can reuse the same background across days.

4. Gamification & retention features
Streaks: "Day 12 of showing up for yourself" — simple counter, resets if they miss a day (or use a forgiving "streak freeze" to avoid punishing tone)
Daily notification: optional email/push at a user-set time
Archive: let users revisit past messages that hit home ("save this one")
Weekly recap: "Here's what motivated you this week" — email digest
5. Ethical/design guardrails
Don't gate motivation behind aggressive notification/dark patterns — respect opt-outs
Be careful with tone around users who might be struggling (avoid toxic positivity, avoid guilt for broken streaks)
If personalizing based on user input, don't store sensitive info unnecessarily
6. MVP scope
Curated bank of ~100 messages, one per day (rotating), no login required, simple streak via localStorage, no personalization yet. Ship that, see if people return, then layer in accounts/personalization/notifications.

